  "summary": "Preprints stress-test perturbation and multi-omics models; benchmarks show reported gains hinge on evaluation design, including OOD and frozen settings.",
  "synthesis": "Activity in this cluster concentrates on validating perturbation and multi-omics models rather than introducing new architectures alone. Several preprints report that claimed gains depend on evaluation design. A benchmark over 600 embedding-task pairs tests whether foundation-model embeddings outperform simple baselines, and a frozen-evaluation study finds pretraining-scale effects visible only without standard finetuning. EL-PERTURB reports that perturbation prediction models fail out-of-distribution, and SABLE-MO shows factor reproducibility alone is insufficient, with stricter retention reducing false acceptance from 33% to 19% in simulations. Newer modeling work includes SynGeneFlow's additive-prior residual flow and DCAF's unified simulation framework. The signal shows a rise ratio of 3.16 across 12 items (10 preprints, 2 published), with no code or industry signals recorded. Two published items extend representation choices: spliced/unspliced NMF and genome-aware semi-supervised DNA classification.",
